      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14184158/assets/472c0aebabed979b97d9d68648785773f0715da9e8cf88c1f1ee912a27d6cb77.png)\n\nThe graph above shows that the distance $d$ an electric train travels is directly proportional to the number of hours $t$ since its journey started. Find a direct variation equation that describes the relationship between $d$ and $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $d$ varies directly with $t,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nd=kt.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice that there is a point on the graph with $t=4$ and $d=840.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}840 & =k\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{840}{4} & =k \\\\ 210 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nd=210t.\n$$",
